== Air Defence ==
{{Main|Myanmar Air Defense Forces}}

The [[Myanmar Air Defense Forces]] ({{lang|my|လေကြောင်းရန်ကာကွယ်ရေးတပ်ဖွဲ့}}) is one of the major branches of the Tatmadaw. It was established as the Air Defence Command in 1997 but was not fully operational until late 1999. It was renamed the Bureau of Air Defence in the early 2000s. In early 2000s, the Tatmadaw established the Myanmar Integrated Air Defence System (MIADS) ({{lang|my|မြန်မာ့အလွှာစုံပေါင်းစပ်လေကြောင်းရန်ကာကွယ်ရေးစနစ်}}) with help from [[Russia]], [[Ukraine]] and [[China]]. It is a tri-service bureau with units from all three branches of the armed forces. All air defence assets except [[anti-aircraft artillery]] are integrated into MIADS.<ref name="Indrastra MIADS">{{cite web|url=https://www.indrastra.com/2015/12/ANALYSIS-Myanmar-Integrated-Air-Defense-System-0516.html?m=1|title=Myanmar Integrated Air Defense System|author=IndraStra Global Editorial Team|access-date=7 December 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201031065753/https://www.indrastra.com/2015/12/ANALYSIS-Myanmar-Integrated-Air-Defense-System-0516.html?m=1|archive-date=31 October 2020|url-status=live|date=30 October 2020}}</ref>

==Military intelligence==
{{Main|Office of the Chief of Military Security Affairs}}

The Office of the Chief of Military Security Affairs (OCMSA), commonly referred to by its Burmese acronym {{lang|my|Sa Ya Pha}} ({{lang|my|စရဖ}}), is a branch of the Myanmar's Armed Forces tasked with intelligence gathering. It was created to replace the Directorate of Defence Services Intelligence (DDSI), which was disbanded in 2004.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Paing|first1=Yan|title=Burmese Military Reshuffle Sees New Security Chief Appointed|url=http://www.irrawaddy.org/burma/burmese-military-reshuffle-sees-new-security-chief-appointed.html|accessdate=2015-08-18|publisher=The Irrawaddy|date=September 9, 2014|ref=1|archive-date=7 December 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151207213125/http://www.irrawaddy.org/burma/burmese-military-reshuffle-sees-new-security-chief-appointed.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Defence industries==
{{Main|Myanmar Directorate of Defence Industries}}

The Myanmar Directorate of Defence Industries (DI) consists of 25 major factories throughout the country that produce approximately 70 major products for Army, Navy and Air Force.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mathieson |first=David Scott |date=2023-01-16 |title=Targeting Myanmar's factories of death |url=https://asiatimes.com/2023/01/targeting-myanmars-factories-of-death/ |access-date=2023-03-29 |website=Asia Times |language=en-US |archive-date=29 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230329055826/https://asiatimes.com/2023/01/targeting-myanmars-factories-of-death/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The main products include [[automatic rifle]]s, [[machine gun]]s, [[sub-machine gun]]s, [[anti-aircraft guns]], complete range of mortar and [[artillery shell|artillery ammunition]], [[aircraft]] and anti-aircraft ammunition, [[tank]] and anti-tank ammunition, [[Bomb|bombs]], [[grenade]]s, [[anti-tank mine]]s, [[anti-personnel mine]]s such as the [[M14 mine|M14]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.karenhumanrightsgroup.org/photoreports/2008photos/gallery2008/section5.html |access-date=2 November 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110501105138/http://www.karenhumanrightsgroup.org/photoreports/2008photos/gallery2008/section5.html |archive-date=1 May 2011 |title=Karen Human Rights Group &#124; KHRG Photo Gallery 2008 &#124; Landmines, mortars, army camps and soldiers }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.atimes.com/atimes/Southeast_Asia/HK04Ae01.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061229045738/http://atimes.com/atimes/Southeast_Asia/HK04Ae01.html|url-status=unfit|archive-date=29 December 2006|title=Asia Times Online :: Southeast Asia news – Myanmar, the world's landmine capital|access-date=29 November 2014}}</ref> [[pyrotechnics]], commercial [[Explosive|explosives]] and [[commercial products]], and [[Rocket|rockets]] and so forth. DI have produced new [[Assault rifle|assault rifles]] and [[Light machine gun|light machine-guns]] for the infantry. The MA series of weapons were designed to replace the old German-designed but locally manufactured [[Heckler & Koch]] [[Heckler & Koch G3|G3s]] and G4s that equipped Burma's army since the 1960s.<ref name=":2" />

